{
"dur": 51.652,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 9.8,
"text": "Around 600 BC, Pharaoh Necho the Second had an idea. Ships could sail from the Mediterranean up the Nile, then along a new canal east to the Red Sea.",
"end": 10.075
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 10.075,
"vo_start": 10.35,
"vo_end": 18.91,
"text": "Thousands of farmers dug with copper tools and reed baskets under the desert sun. The Greek historian Herodotus wrote that a hundred and twenty thousand of them died.",
"end": 19.185
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 19.185,
"vo_start": 19.46,
"vo_end": 24.854,
"text": "Then an oracle warned Necho that the canal would only help foreign invaders. He stopped the work.",
"end": 25.129
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 25.129,
"vo_start": 25.404,
"vo_end": 32.964,
"text": "About a hundred years later, the Persian king Darius, who now ruled Egypt, finished it. His ships could sail from the Nile to the Red Sea.",
"end": 33.239
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 33.239,
"vo_start": 33.514,
"vo_end": 43.032,
"text": "To make sure nobody forgot, he set up giant carved stones along the bank. One says: I ordered this canal to be dug, and ships went from Egypt to Persia, as I desired.",
"end": 43.307
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 43.307,
"vo_start": 43.582,
"vo_end": 50.702,
"text": "But the desert never stops moving. Wind-blown sand and river mud slowly filled the channel, and it had to be dug out again and again.",
"end": 51.652
}
],
